{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 New York Yankees arrived with expectations, and early on they delivered. A 4-1 March and a 16-10 April established the tone: this was a team capable of winning games in dramatic fashion, fueled by unexpected contributors. José Caballero emerged as a surprise catalyst, delivering clutch doubles that swung outcomes, while Amed Rosario's three-run home run on April 7th — a swing worth more than half a win in probability — announced that this roster had depth. Trent Grisham, Ryan McMahon, and Anthony Volpe all contributed signature moments as the Yankees built a comfortable cushion in the standings.\n\nMay and June told a more complicated story. The bullpen, and David Bednar in particular, became a recurring source of heartbreak — surrendering home runs in high-leverage spots that erased leads and flipped games. Despite a solid 16-12 May, the momentum wavered, and a 12-14 June represented the low point of the season. The pitching staff absorbed damage from Milwaukee, Baltimore, and Boston in those weeks, and the offense, while still capable of fireworks like Paul Goldschmidt's ninth homer and Ben Rice's surge, couldn't always compensate for late-inning meltdowns.\n\nJuly and August represented the Yankees' finest stretch. A 14-11 July featured Jazz Chisholm Jr. launching a go-ahead homer worth 0.649 WPA, Ben Rice tripling home two runs in a decisive moment, and the lineup clicking with genuine depth across the order. Luis García Jr. and Ben Rice both delivered clutch hits in August, and the Yankees surged to a 16-12 record that month as the rotation steadied and the offense found rhythm. The 15-8 September finish was dominant — the best monthly record of the year — and carried New York into the postseason with real momentum.\n\nThe playoffs brought a brief, bright moment in the Wild Card before the harsh reality of October set in. The Yankees advanced but ran into the Tampa Bay Rays in the ALDS, where Max Fried was touched for two home runs in Game 2 and the offense couldn't manufacture enough to compensate. The series slipped away in familiar fashion — promising rallies cut short, critical strikeouts, and the margins that separated a 93-win season from a championship run proving razor-thin. It was a season of real accomplishment, genuine drama, and the kind of close-but-not-quite ending that leaves a franchise hungry for more.",
